(a) Grounds. Any of the following shall be grounds for implementation of a fine not to exceed one thousand dollars ($1,000.00), suspension, or revocation of an adult entertainment establishment license:
(1) The making of any material false, misleading, or fraudulent statement in an application or any document submitted in connection with a license issued under this chapter;
(2) A knowing violation by the licensee, operator(s), owner(s), or any employee or agent of the licensed premises of any provision of this chapter, including but not limited to sections 9-12-3, 9-12-4, 9-12-6, 9-12-7, 9-12-10, 9-12-13, or 9-12-14;
(3) The revocation of the employment permits of three (3) operators of the adult entertainment establishment pursuant to subsection 9-12-6(b)(6) of this chapter within any twelve (12) month period;
(4) The conviction of, or plea of guilty or nolo contendere by, the applicant, licensee, operator(s), or owner(s) to any offense that would render such person or establishment ineligible to hold a license under subsection 9-12-5(d)(3);
(5) Failure to maintain compliance with all applicable federal, state, or local laws, including but not limited to zoning, building, fire, and life safety regulations.
(b) Denial; procedure. Within ninety (90) days of actual receipt of a fully completed application for a license to operate an adult entertainment establishment, the chief of police or his designee shall either approve or deny the application. In no event shall the decision whether to approve or deny the adult entertainment establishment license application be withheld for more than ninety (90) days after actual receipt of a fully completed application. In the event that a decision is not rendered within ninety (90) days of receipt of a complete application, the application shall be deemed approved for purposes of commencing operations, subject to continued compliance with this chapter and all applicable laws. The city shall issue an adult entertainment establishment license to an applicant who informs the city, through the city manager or the chief of police, of the fact that an application has been submitted, but no decision has been made thereon for a period of more than ninety (90) days following actual receipt of the fully completed application. Issuance of a license under this subsection shall not preclude subsequent enforcement action, including suspension or revocation, if it is later determined that the application contained false information or that the applicant does not meet the requirements of this chapter. In the event that the chief of police denies an application for an adult entertainment establishment license, then within five (5) business days following such denial, notice of such denial shall be delivered to the applicant in person or deposited in the United States mail, with sufficient first class postage affixed, properly addressed to the applicant at the address provided for such notices on the application.
(Ord. No. 14-25, § 1, 10-27-2014; Ord. No. 26-06, § 4(Exh. D), 5-4-2026; Ord. No. 26-15, § 4(Exh. D), 8-3-2026)

